{'extraction_id': 31, 'ref_id': 'REF-00996', 'slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'parameter_id': 3, 'identity_code': 'MOB', 'jurisdiction': 'BE', 'claim_type': 'qualitative', 'claimed_value': 'physical feasibility for the independent wheelchair user is the starting point', 'claim_text': 'Bij hellingen vormt de fysieke haalbaarheid van de (zelfstandige) rolstoelgebruiker het uitgangspunt.', 'source_section': 'Handboek › Niveauverschillen › Helling — Verhouding percentage-hoogteoverbrugging-lengte', 'root_type': 'committee_assertion', 'root_ref_id': 'REF-00996', 'measurement_paradigm': 'stated_unmeasured', 'device_class': 'manual_self_propelled', 'contested': 0, 'extraction_method': 'full-read', 'extraction_status': 'verified', 'notes': 'THE CRITERION OF ACCEPTABILITY, AND THE ONLY ONE THIS CORPUS HOLDS. Every other row for this parameter is a ceiling asserted with no warrant: the ADA, the Japanese order, AD M, the Flemish decree and the IPC guide all state a number and none says what makes it acceptable. This sentence says what the number is FOR — the physical feasibility of the (independent) wheelchair user — and it is the owner question of 2026-09-16 almost verbatim. It supplies NO value, and is first-class evidence anyway under the 2026-09-13 ruling; figure_role=finding for that reason. device_class=manual_self_propelled is from the source itself, not inferred: zelfstandige rolstoelgebruiker is a self-propelling wheelchair user, and the sentence immediately after names assistant, pram, trolley and rollator as the REFERENCE rather than the criterion, so the design case is explicitly the person propelling themselves. THE CRITERION IS RULED BY A GOVERNMENT AGENCY, NOT BY DISABLED PEOPLE. Inter and Gelijke Kansen in Vlaanderen are Flemish public bodies, so this is a regulator stating what wheelchair users can manage, not wheelchair users saying so. It is the best-warranted statement in the cell and it is still not Co-1.', 'created_at': '2026-09-17 21:51', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-research-batch-12-acceptability-criterion', 'updated_at': '2026-09-17 21:51', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-research-batch-12-acceptability-criterion', 'figure_role': 'finding'}
---
{'extraction_id': 32, 'ref_id': 'REF-00996', 'slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'parameter_id': 3, 'identity_code': 'MOB', 'jurisdiction': 'BE', 'claim_type': 'qualitative', 'claimed_value': 'sustained effort over longer ramps requires a gentler gradient', 'claim_text': 'Hoe groter het hoogteverschil, hoe zachter (kleiner hellingspercentage) de helling moet zijn, omdat men gedurende een langere tijd een inspanning moet leveren, en het dus voor iedereen haalbaar moet blijven de volledige helling te nemen.', 'source_section': 'Handboek › Niveauverschillen › Helling — Verhouding percentage-hoogteoverbrugging-lengte', 'root_type': 'committee_assertion', 'root_ref_id': 'REF-00996', 'measurement_paradigm': 'stated_unmeasured', 'device_class': 'manual_self_propelled', 'contested': 0, 'extraction_method': 'full-read', 'extraction_status': 'verified', 'notes': 'THE MECHANISM BEHIND THE RISE-CONDITIONED SHAPE, STATED OUT LOUD. Batch 11 found three incompatible rule shapes — flat (US/JP), going-conditioned (GB), rise-conditioned (BE-VL) — and could say what each does but not why. This says why the Flemish one is built that way: a greater height means effort sustained for LONGER, so the gradient must fall for the whole ramp to stay feasible. That is a dose-response argument in prose, and it is the same direction the two admitted dose-response studies measure. IT IS ALSO THE MISSING LINK THE OWNER STATEMENT OF 2026-09-16 NAMES. That statement allows dose-response evidence to be reasoned toward a range as a proxy, and warns that monotonic curves with no plateau resolve to 0 degrees, which is not a ramp. This supplies the term that stops the regress: feasibility is not about instantaneous effort but about SUSTAINED effort over the whole run, so the acceptable gradient falls as the run lengthens rather than tending to zero. Still an assertion — no measurement, no citation, no participants — so figure_role=finding and root_type=committee_assertion.', 'created_at': '2026-09-17 21:51', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-research-batch-12-acceptability-criterion', 'updated_at': '2026-09-17 21:51', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-17-research-batch-12-acceptability-criterion', 'figure_role': 'finding'}
---
{'extraction_id': 33, 'ref_id': 'REF-00997', 'slug': 'accessible-circulation-geometry', 'parameter_id': 3, 'identity_code': 'MOB', 'needs_code': 'A-EFFORT', 'jurisdiction': 'AU', 'setting': 'home environment (domiciliary equipment prescription)', 'claim_type': 'qualitative', 'claimed_value': 'the prescriber must assess whether this client can propel over the recommended gradient; some require gentler', 'claim_text': 'Assess the client’s/carer’s ability to propel the wheelchair over this gradient as some clients may require a less steep gradient.', 'source_section': 'Ramp gradient', 'contested': 0, 'extraction_method': 'full-read', 'extraction_status': 'verified', 'notes': 'THE SECOND CRITERION-OF-ACCEPTABILITY STATEMENT IN THE CORPUS, AND THE FIRST PLACING THE DUTY ON A NAMED CLINICIAN. REF-00996 (Flemish handbook) states the criterion as a design principle -- physical feasibility for the independently self-propelling wheelchair user is the starting point. This states it as an OPERATIONAL DUTY: the tabulated gradient is a default and the prescriber must test it against this client. It names no number, so it is a finding and reaches a determination only through the proxy branch (086). SECOND LENS DELIBERATE: A-EFFORT ("cost little energy... rest points", absorbing fatigue, pain, cardiac/respiratory) is what an ability-to-propel assessment is about, and every other parameter-3 extraction carries identity_code MOB and nothing else. R4 asks for the combinatorial dimension; this is one honest step, not a backfill of the other 22 rows. VERBATIM NOTE: claim_text carries U+2019 apostrophes as the CP1252 bytes do; an ASCII-stripped reading rendered them as spaces and was discarded as a decode artefact, not a quotation. [VERBATIM-EXEMPT: ARTEFACT IS PERSISTED BUT NOT UTF-8 DECODABLE. Payload: retrieval-log/session_2026-09-18-research-batch-13-co2-occupational-therapy/1d4ebe3dd57422e2.doc, 159744 bytes, application/msword, HTTP 200 via the R10 Wayback ladder after the live URL returned 403. Legacy OLE compound document encoded CP1252, so the verbatim checker classifies it NOT SEARCHED (not valid UTF-8, no charset declaration) and cannot see text that IS present in the bytes. Re-derivable in one line: open(path,"rb").read().decode("cp1252") then search for the sentence. NOT exempt because the text was unretrievable -- exempt because the checker cannot read a format it was handed. A UTF-8 rendition was deliberately NOT generated to satisfy the gate: an artefact this session wrote itself would make the check circular.]', 'created_at': '2026-09-18 01:48', 'created_by_session': 'session_2026-09-18-research-batch-13-co2-occupational-therapy', 'updated_at': '2026-09-18 01:48', 'updated_by_session': 'session_2026-09-18-research-batch-13-co2-occupational-therapy', 'figure_role': 'finding'}
